## MacinTalk — `pyretrotts/_backend.py` and the rest of the core

Apple Computer's MacinTalk 2/3, ported from C sources whose headers read:

    File:       FrontEnd.c
    Contains:   Implementation of MacInTalk2 FrontEnd
    Written by: Tim Schaaff
    Copyright:  (c) 1991-1992 by Apple Computer, Inc., all rights reserved.

and whose version string reads `1.4d7, (c) Apple Computer, Inc. 1994`.

**Status: Apple retains copyright.** The C sources circulate publicly and no
grant of licence accompanies them. The Python here is a translation of that C
and is therefore a derivative work. The tables in `pyretrotts/_data.py` are
extracted from Apple's `Data.c` and `Sounds.c`.

Apple has not, to this project's knowledge, released MacinTalk under any open
licence. Treat redistribution as unresolved.

## DECtalk — `pyretrotts/_dectalk.py`, `pyretrotts/translate.py`

Digital Equipment Corporation's DECtalk. What lives in this repository today is
**only the markup dialect**: the `[: ]` command syntax, the phoneme mnemonics,
and the `<duration,pitch>` singing notation. No DECtalk engine code, no DECtalk
tables, and no DECtalk dictionary are present.

A markup syntax is a description of an interface, not an implementation, and the
mapping from mnemonic to phoneme is a fact about the notation. Nothing was
copied.

**Status: no DECtalk code is vendored.** If the engine itself is ported (see
`docs/dectalk-port-plan.md`) the source is the Fonix/Force release at
`github.com/dectalk/dectalk`, whose own licence terms must be read and recorded
here before any of it lands.

## SAM (Software Automatic Mouth) — planned, not yet present

Don't Ask Software, 1982. The intended source is `github.com/vidarh/SAM`, a C
reimplementation produced by translating the original 6502 assembler
opcode-by-opcode.

**Status: encumbered, and knowingly so.** That repository ships no LICENSE file.
Its README states that the copyright holder is **SoftVoice, Inc.**, and that its
author "cannot put my code under any specific open source software license."
Being a translation of a disassembly makes it a derivative work of SoftVoice's
program; it does not make it free.

The age of the work and the state of the company do not change this. Copyright
does not lapse on abandonment, and it does not lapse when a company dissolves —
it is an asset that transfers, and it runs for decades yet.

Any SAM port here is therefore:

- kept in its own subpackage, not mixed into the MIT-licensed core;
- **not** covered by this project's MIT licence;
- not published to PyPI or any other distribution channel without an explicit,
  separate decision to do so.

## The song scores — `songs/`

DECtalk scores of traditional and public-domain songs: sea shanties, nursery
rhymes, and carols long out of copyright. The wider corpus these were selected
from consists largely of transcriptions of commercial recordings, and is
deliberately not included.

